<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mustafa Barzani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1903–1979</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mustafa Barzani was a Kurdish nationalist leader who led the Kurdish movement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> for much of the 20th century. He commanded the army of the short-lived Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabad">Mahabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1946, then led his followers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, returning to Iraq in 1958. As head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> he fought the Iraqi government in the 1960s and 1970s, until Iranian support ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> Agreement of 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-barzani.jpg" alt="Mustafa Barzani" width="269" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Барзанский фотограф. Original uploader was Павел Шехтман at ru.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mustafa_Barzani_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
